<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 17" label="MODULE 17 / SMALL NETWORK DESIGN AND TROUBLESHOOTING" /><h1>Build the network.<br /><span>Prove it works.</span></h1><p>A small network combines endpoint devices, switches, routers, wireless access, addressing, and application services. A reliable build includes a clear plan and a repeatable verification process.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Plan the small network</h2><p>Identify the required users, applications, devices, addressing, wireless coverage, Internet access, and security needs. Select suitable switches, routers, access points, and media. Use a logical topology to document VLANs, subnets, interfaces, and default gateways, and a physical topology to document device placement and cabling.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1419 · IMG-1399 · IMG-1472</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Scale without losing control</h2><p>As a network grows, use hierarchical design, structured addressing, documented configurations, and consistent security policy. Plan for additional users, services, and traffic. Standardized naming, configuration backups, and monitoring make a network easier to maintain and troubleshoot.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1426 · IMG-1413</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Use a six-step troubleshooting method</h2><p>A repeatable process prevents random configuration changes and makes the result easier to verify:</p><ol><li><strong>Identify the problem.</strong> Gather symptoms from the user, define the scope, and establish what is and is not working.</li><li><strong>Establish a theory of probable causes.</strong> Use the evidence to list likely explanations, starting with the simplest relevant fault domain.</li><li><strong>Test the theory.</strong> Run a targeted test. If it does not confirm the theory, gather more information and revise it.</li><li><strong>Establish a plan and implement the solution.</strong> Choose a corrective action based on the confirmed cause, then apply it deliberately. If the required decision, expertise, or network access is outside your authority, follow policy and escalate instead of improvising.</li><li><strong>Verify the solution and apply preventive measures.</strong> Test full functionality, including the original failing service, and reduce the chance of recurrence where practical.</li><li><strong>Document findings and outcomes.</strong> Record symptoms, evidence, cause, actions, and verification results.</li></ol><p>Work from the local connection outward: physical link and interface state, host addressing, default gateway, routed path, DNS, and finally the application. Do not assume a failed ping alone identifies the faulty device.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1428 · IMG-1407 · IMG-1423 · IMG-1466 · IMG-1468 · IMG-1481 · IMG-1482</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Compare performance with a network baseline</h2><p>A baseline records normal network behavior so current measurements have context. Compare the same endpoints or path under similar time-of-day and workload conditions. For example, a documented H1-to-H3 baseline of <code>36/97/132 ms</code> (minimum/average/maximum) compared with a later result of <code>1458/2390/6066 ms</code> shows severe added delay even though replies are still received. Connectivity exists, but performance is no longer normal.</p><p>When planning an upgrade, capture traffic during peak utilization and on multiple network segments. Some traffic is local to a particular segment and will not appear in a capture elsewhere. Analyze source, destination, and traffic type to document application and service requirements instead of estimating capacity from a single speed test.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Network baseline and traffic analysis</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">05</div><div><h2>Choose IOS commands that answer the question</h2><p>Use concise verification commands before changing configuration. <code>show running-config</code> verifies the active configuration; <code>show interfaces</code> reports interface state and errors; <code>show ip interface</code> and <code>show ip interface brief</code> expose Layer 3 addressing and status; <code>show ip route</code> displays the routing table; <code>show arp</code> lists known IPv4-to-MAC mappings; <code>show protocols</code> reports operational protocols; and <code>show version</code> reports platform, memory, interface, and software information. For IPv6 addresses, use <code>show ipv6 interface</code>. Use <code>show cdp neighbors detail</code> when you need neighbor addressing and device details, not just the local port mapping.</p><p>IOS extended ping and extended traceroute let an administrator choose a source address or interface. This matters when testing reachability from the same interface a remote host uses, especially when the return path may be asymmetric. Start the interactive extended mode by entering <code>ping</code> or <code>traceroute</code> without a destination in privileged EXEC mode.</p><p>IOS ping output also provides clues: <code>!</code> indicates an echo reply, <code>.</code> indicates a timeout, and <code>U</code> indicates that a router returned an ICMP destination-unreachable message. A timeout means the probe did not receive a reply; it does not, by itself, identify which hop or device caused the failure.</p><p>Use <code>debug</code> commands only for a specific troubleshooting target. On a VTY session, <code>terminal monitor</code> enables terminal display of log messages. Narrow debug output where possible, and stop it with the matching <code>no debug</code> command or <code>undebug all</code>. Debug output can consume substantial router resources, so avoid leaving it enabled.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Host and IOS commands</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">06</div><div><h2>Scale using documented requirements</h2><p>Before adding capacity, maintain both physical and logical topology diagrams, a device inventory, and an itemized IT budget. Analyze traffic by protocol, application, service, and network segment so equipment and link upgrades match actual requirements. These records make growth decisions more deliberate and help prevent avoidable bottlenecks.</p><p>Track employee endpoint utilization over time as well. A useful snapshot includes each device's operating system and version, CPU, RAM, and drive utilization, plus the network and non-network applications in use. Comparing snapshots across employees and over time helps identify evolving protocol requirements and associated traffic flows before deciding whether network capacity or services need to change.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Small network growth and employee utilization</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">07</div><div><h2>Worked VLSM plan for the Skills Integration Challenge</h2><p>The source challenge allocates <code>192.168.0.0/24</code> to four networks: 100 staff hosts, 50 Sales hosts, 25 IT hosts, and 25 future Guest hosts. Allocate the largest subnet first. The following is one valid, contiguous plan that meets each host requirement:</p><div class="address-plan"><table><thead><tr><th>Network</th><th>Hosts</th><th>Prefix</th><th>Network address</th><th>Usable range</th><th>Broadcast</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Staff</td><td>100</td><td><code>/25</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.0</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.1–126</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.127</code></td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td>50</td><td><code>/26</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.128</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.129–190</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.191</code></td></tr><tr><td>IT</td><td>25</td><td><code>/27</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.192</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.193–222</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.223</code></td></tr><tr><td>Guest</td><td>25</td><td><code>/27</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.224</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.225–254</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.255</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="lab-note">A <code>/25</code> provides 126 usable addresses, a <code>/26</code> provides 62, and each <code>/27</code> provides 30. This allocation matches the address blocks in the Packet Tracer 9.0.1 activity's embedded comparison/answer state. The Guest subnet is reserved for future use; the activity does not ask you to configure a Guest device.</p><h3>Verified endpoint and switch-management addresses</h3><div class="address-plan"><table><thead><tr><th>Network</th><th>R1 gateway</th><th>PC address</th><th>Switch SVI</th><th>IPv6 prefix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Staff</td><td><code>192.168.0.1</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.3</code></td><td>S1 <code>192.168.0.2</code></td><td><code>2001:db8:acad::/64</code></td></tr><tr><td>Sales</td><td><code>192.168.0.129</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.131</code></td><td>S2 <code>192.168.0.130</code></td><td><code>2001:db8:acad:1::/64</code></td></tr><tr><td>IT</td><td><code>192.168.0.193</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.195</code></td><td>S3 <code>192.168.0.194</code></td><td><code>2001:db8:acad:2::/64</code></td></tr><tr><td>Guest</td><td>Not assigned</td><td>Future</td><td>Not assigned</td><td>Not assigned</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Each PC uses its R1 address as the IPv4 default gateway. The three PCs use IPv6 host ID <code>::2</code>, link-local address <code>FE80::2</code>, and link-local default gateway <code>FE80::1</code>. R1 uses <code>FE80::1</code> on each LAN interface. The DNS server is <code>64.100.0.2</code> and <code>2001:db8:cafe::2</code>; the Web server is <code>64.100.0.3/29</code> and <code>2001:db8:cafe::3/64</code>.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Packet Tracer Skills Integration Challenge</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">08</div><div><h2>Support voice and video applications</h2><p>Real-time services such as voice calls and streaming video depend on network capacity and predictable delivery. The network administrator must confirm that the infrastructure can support the required applications and configure appropriate priority treatment. Quality of Service (QoS) mechanisms help reduce latency problems by managing how traffic is treated during congestion.</p><p><strong>VoIP</strong> is typically less expensive than a full IP telephony deployment, but the source notes that this can come with trade-offs in quality and features. <strong>IP telephony</strong> uses dedicated servers for call control and signaling. Real-Time Transport Protocol (RTP) and Real-Time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP) support real-time applications.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Small Network Applications and Protocols</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">09</div><div><h2>Apply the activity's router and switch security requirements</h2><p>The R1 task combines management-plane hardening with IPv4/IPv6 routing. Set the hostname, disable DNS lookup, configure an enable secret, encrypt plaintext line passwords, and enforce the ten-character minimum. Configure the MOTD warning, then create the local administrator and SSH identity:</p><pre class="command-example"><code>hostname R1
no ip domain-lookup
service password-encryption
security passwords min-length 10
enable secret Ciscoenpa55
login block-for 180 attempts 4 within 120
banner motd #Warning: Unauthorized access prohibited#
ip domain-name CCNA-lab.com
username Admin1 privilege 15 secret Admin1pa55
crypto key generate rsa modulus 1024
line con 0
 password Ciscoconpa55
 login
 exec-timeout 5 0
line vty 0 4
 exec-timeout 5 0
 login local
 transport input ssh</code></pre><p>Configure the console with the activity's console password, <code>login</code>, and <code>exec-timeout 5 0</code>. On VTY lines 0–4, use <code>login local</code>, <code>transport input ssh</code>, and <code>exec-timeout 5 0</code>. Configure the router's three Gigabit Ethernet interfaces with the addresses above, the matching IPv6 global addresses, link-local address <code>FE80::1</code>, and <code>no shutdown</code>. Enable IPv6 routing with <code>ipv6 unicast-routing</code>. The activity's answer state also has an IPv4 default route through <code>172.16.1.1</code> and an IPv6 default route through <code>2001:DB8:2::2</code>; the Central router and ISP cluster are already configured.</p><p>For each 2960 switch, configure its hostname, <code>no ip domain-lookup</code>, <code>service password-encryption</code>, an enable secret, and its VLAN 1 SVI address/default gateway from the table. Set the console password, enable <code>login</code>, and use <code>exec-timeout 5 0</code> on the console and VTY lines. The activity requires the five-minute VTY timeout; it does not require SSH on the switches.</p><p class="lab-note"><strong>Source discrepancy to watch:</strong> the written activity instructions explicitly require Admin1 to have privilege level 15 and a 1024-bit RSA key. The PKA 9.0.1 comparison tree stores the Admin1 username/secret and SSH transport settings, but does not expose a separate privilege-15 or RSA-modulus check. Follow the written instructions; do not omit <code>privilege 15</code> or the 1024-bit key just because those fields are not separately represented in the visible comparator.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Packet Tracer 9.0.1 · 17.8.2 Skills Integration Challenge · comparison tree and instruction page</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-lab"><div class="lesson-number">LAB</div><div><h2>Follow a repeatable troubleshooting path</h2><p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A user can reach a public IP address but cannot open a website by name. Start with the endpoint and move outward.</p><ol><li>On Windows, run <code>ipconfig /all</code>. Check the IPv4 address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server. An address in <code>169.254.0.0/16</code> suggests the host did not obtain a DHCP lease.</li><li>Run <code>ping 127.0.0.1</code> to test the local TCP/IP stack, then ping the host's own address and default gateway.</li><li>Run <code>ping 1.1.1.1</code>. If this succeeds but names fail, basic IP connectivity is working and DNS becomes a strong suspect.</li><li>Run <code>nslookup example.com</code> to query the configured DNS server. Check the server address and whether the query times out or returns an incorrect result.</li><li>Use <code>tracert 1.1.1.1</code> to inspect the path if remote connectivity is failing. A timeout at one intermediate hop is not proof of a forwarding failure if later hops still respond.</li></ol><p class="lab-note">On Cisco IOS, pair host tests with <code>show ip interface brief</code>, <code>show interfaces</code>, and <code>show ip route</code>. Verify the fix with the original failing application, not just a successful ping.</p></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Build a Small Network</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
